	Approach 3: Zero-copy data plane with Apache Arrow
	A recent research paper (AAFLOW) explicitly addresses the serialization overhead problem in agentic/guardrail pipelines. citeturn40search12
	The idea
		Use Apache Arrow's columnar format as the shared memory representation across ALL pipeline stages — zero serialization, zero copy.
		How it works
		Stage 1 (Input Guardrail):
  - Reads Arrow buffer (zero copy)
  - Writes result to Arrow buffer (zero copy)
		Stage 2 (LLM Inference):
  - Reads tokens from Arrow buffer
  - DLPack or CUDA IPC to GPU (minimal copy)
  - Writes output to Arrow buffer
		Stage 3 (Output Guardrail):
  - Reads Arrow buffer (zero copy)
  - Writes final result
		The paper reports: "up to 4.64× pipeline speedup and 2.8× gains in embedding and upsert phases" — not from faster models, but from eliminating serialization and communication overhead. citeturn40search12
		Practical implementation
		
		
		
		Python
		import pyarrow as pa
		
		# Shared Arrow buffer — no serialization between stages
		batch = pa.record_batch({
