The situation
I was brought in as a System Engineer to design and build the real-time fraud scoring infrastructure for a large payment processor handling ~12,000 transactions per second across 3 geographic regions. The existing system was a legacy Java microservices stack running on generic Kubernetes with ~35 ms P99 latency for fraud scoring. Business required sub-10 ms P99 end-to-end for the hot-path fraud decision (feature extraction → XGBoost prediction → rule engine → decision) to meet card-network SLA requirements and reduce false declines.
The scoring pipeline was:
	• Feature extraction: aggregate 15 features from transaction history (velocity, spend patterns, merchant risk)
	• Model inference: XGBoost ensemble (CPU) + a small transformer classifier (GPU)
	• Rule engine: 200+ business rules post-model
	• Decision: approve / decline / step-up authentication
	My job was to redesign the infrastructure from host level through Kubernetes through networking through GPU to hit the latency target without sacrificing throughput.
	
	Phase 1: Host-level foundation (bare-metal nodes)
	CPU isolation and NUMA pinning
	The first thing I did was eliminate noisy-neighbor interference at the kernel level. Our bare-metal nodes were dual-socket systems (2× Intel Xeon 8380, 40 cores each, 2 NUMA nodes).
	I configured CPU isolation to reserve cores exclusively for the fraud scoring workload:
	
	
	
	Shell
	# /etc/default/grub — kernel boot parameters
	GRUB_CMDLINE_LINUX="isolcpus=4-19,24-39 nohz_full=4-19,24-39 rcu_nocbs=4-19,24-39"
	Show more lines
	This did three things:
		○ isolcpus removed cores 4–19 and 24–39 from the general scheduler, so no OS processes or other containers could land on them
		○ nohz_full disabled timer interrupts on those cores (adaptive-ticks), eliminating periodic jitter
		○ rcu_nocbs offloaded RCU callbacks from isolated cores, removing another source of unpredictable latency spikes
		I verified isolation with:
		
		
		
	Shell
	cat /sys/devices/system/cpu/isolated
	# 4-19,24-39
	Show more lines
	For the fraud scoring service, I pinned:
		○ Feature extraction threads → cores 4–11 (NUMA node 0, close to NIC)
		○ Model inference threads → cores 12–19 (NUMA node 0, close to GPU)
		○ Rule engine threads → cores 24–31 (NUMA node 1)
		The NUMA pinning was critical because our NIC was on PCIe bus attached to NUMA node 0, and the GPU (NVIDIA L4) was also on NUMA node 0. Crossing NUMA boundaries for hot-path memory access would add ~100 ns per access, which at thousands of accesses per request compounds to microseconds of waste.
		I validated NUMA topology with:
		
		
		
	Shell
	numactl --hardware
	lstopo-no-graphics
	Show more lines
	HugePage configuration
	Next, I configured HugePages to reduce TLB (Translation Lookahead Buffer) misses. The fraud scoring service maintained large in-memory feature stores (~8 GB per node) and model weights (~2 GB). With standard 4 KB pages, these structures span millions of page table entries, causing frequent TLB misses.
	I allocated 2 MB HugePages at boot:
	
	
	
	Shell
	# /etc/default/grub
	GRUB_CMDLINE_LINUX="... default_hugepagesz=2M hugepagesz=2M hugepages=8192"
	Show more lines
	This gave us 16 GB of HugePage-backed memory. I also reserved some 1 GB HugePages for the XGBoost model's feature matrix, which was a single contiguous allocation:
	
	
	
	Shell
	# /etc/sysctl.d/hugepages.conf
	vm.nr_hugepages = 8192
	vm.hugetlb_shm_group = 1000
	Show more lines
	The impact was measurable: TLB miss rate dropped from ~4.2% to ~0.3% on the feature extraction path, verified with perf stat:
	
	
	
	Shell
	perf stat -e dTLB-load-misses,dTLB-loads -p <pid>
	Show more lines
	Kernel tuning
	I tuned several kernel parameters for low-latency behavior:
	
	
	
	Shell
	# /etc/sysctl.d/99-lowlatency.conf
	
	# Disable swap entirely (latency poison)
	vm.swappiness = 0
	
	# Increase network buffer sizes (for burst traffic)
	net.core.rmem_max = 16777216
	net.core.wmem_max = 16777216
	net.ipv4.tcp_rmem = 4096 87380 16777216
	net.ipv4.tcp_wmem = 4096 65536 16777216
	
	# Reduce TCP latency
	net.ipv4.tcp_nodelay = 1
	net.ipv4.tcp_low_latency = 1
	
	# Increase somaxconn for burst handling
	net.core.somaxconn = 65535
	net.core.netdev_max_backlog = 65535
	
	# Disable transparent hugepages (causes compaction stalls)
	# Note: We use EXPLICIT hugepages, not THP
	echo never > /sys/kernel/mm/transparent_hugepage/enabled
	Show more lines
	I explicitly disabled Transparent HugePages (THP) because THP's background compaction daemon (khugepaged) causes unpredictable latency spikes — exactly the opposite of what we want. We used explicit HugePages instead, which are pre-allocated and never compacted.
	IRQ affinity for NIC interrupts
	Our fraud scoring traffic came through a Mellanox ConnectX-6 NIC with 16 RX queues. By default, the kernel distributes NIC interrupts across all CPUs, including our isolated cores. This caused interrupt storms on latency-sensitive cores.
	I pinned NIC IRQs to non-isolated housekeeping cores (cores 0–3, 20–23):
	
	
	
	Shell
	# Disable irqbalance (it fights manual affinity)
	systemctl disable irqbalance
	systemctl stop irqbalance
	
	# Pin each NIC IRQ to housekeeping cores
	for irq in $(grep mlx5 /proc/interrupts | awk '{print $1}' | tr -d ':'); do
	 echo 0-3,20-23 > /proc/irq/$irq/smp_affinity_list
	done
	Show more lines
	I also configured NIC ring buffer and multi-queue:
	
	
	
	Shell
	# Increase ring buffer for burst absorption
	ethtool -G eth0 rx 8192 tx 8192
	
	# Enable multi-queue (already 16 queues on ConnectX-6)
	ethtool -l eth0
	# Combined: 16
	
	# Enable RFS (Receive Flow Steering) for socket-to-queue affinity
	echo 32768 > /proc/sys/net/core/rps_sock_flow_entries
	for f in /sys/class/net/eth0/queues/rx-*/rps_flow_cnt; do
	 echo 2048 > $f
	done
	Show more lines
	PREEMPT_RT consideration
	I evaluated PREEMPT_RT kernel patches for guaranteed worst-case latency. After testing, I found that PREEMPT_RT reduced P99.9 jitter from ~800 µs to ~50 µs, but added ~3% overhead to P50 throughput due to the fully preemptible kernel. Given our SLA was P99 (not P99.9), I chose to not use PREEMPT_RT in production but kept it as a fallback option for future tightening. The isolcpus + nohz_full + IRQ affinity combination was sufficient for our P99 target.
	
