Story 1 (C++): "Building the Zero-Copy, NUMA-Aware Hot-Path Feature Extraction and Scoring Engine Inside Sentinel Gateway"

The problem
Sentinel Gateway's hot path (Tier 0) needed to execute feature extraction + XGBoost scoring + transformer classifier inference in < 10 ms P99. The existing implementation was a Python service that:
	• Deserialized JSON transaction data into Python dicts
	• Computed 15 features by querying Redis and iterating over transaction history
	• Serialized features into a NumPy array
	• Copied the array to GPU for the transformer classifier
	• Copied the result back
	• Serialized the decision into JSON
	The Python path was consuming ~18 ms P99 — nearly all of it in serialization, memory allocation, GIL contention, and cross-boundary copies. The actual compute (feature aggregation + XGBoost predict + transformer forward pass) was < 3 ms. The rest was "glue tax."
	My task: rewrite the hot-path data pipeline in C++ as a shared library (.so) callable from the Rust Sentinel Gateway via FFI, eliminating all unnecessary copies, allocations, and serialization.
	
	Phase 1: NUMA-aware memory architecture
	The memory layout problem
	Our bare-metal nodes were dual-socket (2 NUMA nodes). The NIC was on NUMA node 0, the GPU was on NUMA node 0, but the default malloc allocator scattered pages across both NUMA nodes based on Linux's first-touch policy. This meant:
		○ Transaction data arrived on NUMA 0 (NIC DMA)
		○ Feature computation might touch NUMA 1 pages (~100 ns extra per access)
		○ GPU DMA (PCIe) was fastest from NUMA 0 memory
		○ Every cross-NUMA access added latency that compounded across thousands of memory touches per request
		What I built: NUMA-pinned memory arena
		I implemented a custom memory arena that pre-allocates large blocks from a specific NUMA node using libnuma, then sub-allocates from those blocks with zero system calls on the hot path.
		
		
		
		C++
		#include <numa.h>
		#include <cstddef>
		#include <cstdint>
		#include <atomic>
		
		class NumaArena {
		public:
		 // Allocate arena_size bytes on the specified NUMA node
		 // using 2MB hugepages for TLB efficiency
		 NumaArena(int numa_node, size_t arena_size)
		 : numa_node_(numa_node)
		 , arena_size_(arena_size)
		 , offset_(0)
		 {
		 // Allocate on specific NUMA node with hugepage backing
		 base_ = static_cast<uint8_t*>(
		 numa_alloc_onnode(arena_size_, numa_node_)
		 );
		 if (!base_) {
		 throw std::bad_alloc();
		 }
		
		 // Advise kernel to use hugepages
		 madvise(base_, arena_size_, MADV_HUGEPAGE);
		
		 // Pre-fault all pages to avoid page faults on hot path
		 // (touch every page so the kernel actually maps them)
		 for (size_t i = 0; i < arena_size_; i += 4096) {
		 base_[i] = 0;
		 }
		 }
		
		 ~NumaArena() {
		 numa_free(base_, arena_size_);
		 }
		
		 // Hot-path allocation: bump pointer, no syscall, no lock
		 // Aligned to cache line (64 bytes) to prevent false sharing
		 void* allocate(size_t size) {
		 size = (size + 63) & ~63; // align to 64-byte cache line
		 size_t current = offset_.load(std::memory_order_relaxed);
		 size_t next = current + size;
		
		 if (next > arena_size_) return nullptr; // arena exhausted
		
		 // Single-threaded per-core arena: no CAS needed
		 offset_.store(next, std::memory_order_relaxed);
		 return base_ + current;
		 }
		
		 // Reset arena for next request batch (O(1), no free per object)
		 void reset() {
		 offset_.store(0, std::memory_order_relaxed);
		 }
		
		private:
		 uint8_t* base_;
		 int numa_node_;
		 size_t arena_size_;
		 std::atomic<size_t> offset_;
		};
		Show more lines
		Key design decisions:
			1. numa_alloc_onnode ensures all pages are on the correct NUMA node — no accidental cross-NUMA access
			2. MADV_HUGEPAGE + pre-faulting eliminates TLB misses and page faults on the hot path
			3. Bump-pointer allocation is O(1) with zero fragmentation — no malloc overhead
			4. Per-core arenas (each processing thread gets its own arena) — no locks, no atomic CAS, no contention
			5. reset() instead of per-object free() — the entire arena is recycled per request batch, which is safe because request data is short-lived
			How this connected to Sentinel
			Each Sentinel Gateway worker thread (pinned to a specific CPU core on NUMA node 0) got its own NumaArena. All transaction parsing, feature computation, and intermediate buffers allocated from this arena. When the request completed, one reset() call recycled everything.
			
			Phase 2: Zero-copy transaction parsing with SIMD
			The parsing problem
			Each incoming transaction was an ISO 8583–like message (fixed-length fields with variable-length sub-fields). The Python implementation used json.loads() which:
				□ Allocated dozens of Python objects per transaction
				□ Copied every field into a new Python string
				□ Triggered garbage collection unpredictably
				What I built: SIMD-accelerated zero-copy parser
				Instead of deserializing into objects, I built a parser that produces field pointers (offset + length) into the original receive buffer — the actual field data is never copied.
				
				
				
				C++
				#include <immintrin.h> // AVX2 intrinsics
				#include <cstring>
				
				struct FieldView {
				 const char* data; // pointer into original buffer (zero-copy)
				 uint32_t length;
				};
				
				struct TransactionView {
				 FieldView pan; // Primary Account Number
				 FieldView amount;
				 FieldView merchant_id;
				 FieldView merchant_category;
				 FieldView timestamp;
				 FieldView terminal_id;
				 FieldView country_code;
				 // ... 20+ fields, all zero-copy views into the original buffer
				};
				
				// SIMD-accelerated delimiter search
				// Finds the position of delimiter in buffer using AVX2
				// Processes 32 bytes per iteration instead of 1
				inline size_t simd_find_delimiter(const char* buf, size_t len, char delim) {
				 const __m256i delim_vec = _mm256_set1_epi8(delim);
				 size_t i = 0;
				
				 for (; i + 32 <= len; i += 32) {
				 __m256i chunk = _mm256_loadu_si256(
				 reinterpret_cast<const __m256i*>(buf + i)
				 );
				 __m256i cmp = _mm256_cmpeq_epi8(chunk, delim_vec);
				 uint32_t mask = _mm256_movemask_epi8(cmp);
				 if (mask != 0) {
				 return i + __builtin_ctz(mask); // position of first match
				 }
				 }
				
				 // Scalar fallback for remaining bytes
				 for (; i < len; i++) {
				 if (buf[i] == delim) return i;
				 }
				 return len; // not found
				}
				
				// Parse transaction: produces FieldView pointers into original buffer
				// ZERO allocations, ZERO copies
				bool parse_transaction(const char* raw_buf, size_t raw_len,
				 TransactionView* out) {
				 size_t pos = 0;
				
				 // Field 1: PAN (delimited by |)
				 size_t end = simd_find_delimiter(raw_buf + pos, raw_len - pos, '|');
				 out->pan = { raw_buf + pos, static_cast<uint32_t>(end) };
				 pos += end + 1;
				
				 // Field 2: Amount
				 end = simd_find_delimiter(raw_buf + pos, raw_len - pos, '|');
				 out->amount = { raw_buf + pos, static_cast<uint32_t>(end) };
				 pos += end + 1;
				
				 // ... remaining fields follow same pattern
				 // Each field is a VIEW into raw_buf, not a copy
				
				 return true;
				}
				Show more lines
				Why SIMD matters here:
					® Each transaction has ~20 fields, each delimited
					® Scalar search: 20 × avg 15 bytes = ~300 byte-by-byte comparisons
					® AVX2 search: processes 32 bytes per instruction → ~10 iterations total
					® At 12,000 TPS, this saves ~3.6 million byte comparisons per second
					® More importantly: predictable latency (no branch mispredictions in the SIMD path)
					Integration with NUMA arena
					The TransactionView struct itself was allocated from the per-core NumaArena. The raw receive buffer was already on NUMA node 0 (NIC DMA landed there). The FieldView pointers pointed directly into that buffer. Zero copies, zero allocations on the hot path.
					
					Phase 3: Lock-free SPSC queue for pipeline stages
					The pipeline problem
					The hot path had three stages:
						1. Parse + Feature Extract (CPU-bound)
						2. XGBoost Score (CPU-bound)
						3. Transformer Classify (GPU-bound)
						These ran on different cores (and stage 3 on the GPU). The Python implementation used threading.Queue with mutex locks, causing:
							} Lock contention at high throughput
							} Priority inversion
							} Unpredictable tail latency
							What I built: Lock-free SPSC ring buffer
							Each pipeline stage pair communicated through a single-producer single-consumer (SPSC) lock-free ring buffer — the fastest possible inter-thread communication primitive.
							
							
							
							C++
							#include <atomic>
							#include <cstddef>
							#include <new>
							
							// Cache line size for padding
							static constexpr size_t CACHE_LINE = 64;
							
							template <typename T, size_t Capacity>
							class SPSCQueue {
							 static_assert((Capacity & (Capacity - 1)) == 0,
							 "Capacity must be power of 2");
							
							public:
							 SPSCQueue() : head_(0), tail_(0) {}
							
							 // Producer: try to push (called from one thread only)
							 bool try_push(const T& item) {
							 const size_t head = head_.load(std::memory_order_relaxed);
							 const size_t next = (head + 1) & (Capacity - 1);
							
							 // Check if queue is full
							 if (next == tail_.load(std::memory_order_acquire)) {
							 return false; // queue full — apply backpressure
							 }
							
							 slots_[head] = item;
							 head_.store(next, std::memory_order_release);
							 return true;
							 }
							
							 // Consumer: try to pop (called from one thread only)
							 bool try_pop(T& item) {
							 const size_t tail = tail_.load(std::memory_order_relaxed);
							
							 // Check if queue is empty
							 if (tail == head_.load(std::memory_order_acquire)) {
							 return false; // queue empty
							 }
							
							 item = slots_[tail];
							 const size_t next = (tail + 1) & (Capacity - 1);
							 tail_.store(next, std::memory_order_release);
							 return true;
							 }
							
							private:
							 // Pad head and tail to separate cache lines
							 // Prevents false sharing between producer and consumer
							 alignas(CACHE_LINE) std::atomic<size_t> head_;
							 char pad1_[CACHE_LINE - sizeof(std::atomic<size_t>)];
							
							 alignas(CACHE_LINE) std::atomic<size_t> tail_;
							 char pad2_[CACHE_LINE - sizeof(std::atomic<size_t>)];
							
							 alignas(CACHE_LINE) T slots_[Capacity];
							};
							Show more lines
							Critical design details:
								1. Power-of-2 capacity → bitwise AND replaces modulo (branchless wrap-around)
								2. std::memory_order_acquire/release → minimum necessary ordering (no full barriers, no seq_cst)
								3. Cache-line padding between head_ and tail_ → eliminates false sharing (producer writes head_, consumer writes tail_ — on different cache lines, so no ping-ponging)
								4. No locks, no mutexes, no condition variables → pure atomic loads/stores
								5. Bounded capacity → natural backpressure (if queue is full, producer gets false → Sentinel returns 429)
								Pipeline wiring
								
								
								
								C++
								// Per-core pipeline (all on NUMA node 0)
								
								// Stage 1 → Stage 2: parsed transaction + features
								SPSCQueue<FeatureVector*, 1024> parse_to_score_queue;
								
								// Stage 2 → Stage 3: scored transaction (needs GPU classifier)
								SPSCQueue<ScoredTransaction*, 512> score_to_gpu_queue;
								
								// Stage 3 → Response: final decision
								SPSCQueue<Decision*, 512> gpu_to_response_queue;
								Show more lines
								Each queue carried pointers to arena-allocated objects (not the objects themselves), so the queue slots were only 8 bytes each — the actual data stayed in NUMA-local memory.
								
								Phase 4: Apache Arrow integration for zero-copy publish boundary
								The publish boundary problem
								After the hot-path decision, Sentinel needed to publish the transaction + features + decision to downstream consumers (audit log, retraining pipeline, analytics). The Python implementation serialized everything to JSON and published to Kafka — adding ~3 ms of serialization overhead per request.
								What I built: Arrow IPC zero-copy publish
								I structured the hot-path output as an Apache Arrow RecordBatch that was built in-place during processing (not serialized after the fact).
								
								
								
								C++
								#include <arrow/api.h>
								#include <arrow/ipc/api.h>
								#include <arrow/io/api.h>
								
								class ArrowPublisher {
								public:
								 ArrowPublisher(int numa_node, size_t batch_size)
								 : batch_size_(batch_size)
								 , count_(0)
								 {
								 // Pre-allocate Arrow builders on the correct NUMA node
								 // Arrow supports custom memory pools
								 pool_ = std::make_shared<NumaMemoryPool>(numa_node);
								
								 transaction_id_builder_ =
								 std::make_unique<arrow::StringBuilder>(pool_.get());
								 amount_builder_ =
								 std::make_unique<arrow::DoubleBuilder>(pool_.get());
								 risk_score_builder_ =
								 std::make_unique<arrow::FloatBuilder>(pool_.get());
								 decision_builder_ =
								 std::make_unique<arrow::Int8Builder>(pool_.get());
								 latency_us_builder_ =
								 std::make_unique<arrow::Int64Builder>(pool_.get());
								 // ... more fields for audit envelope
								 }
								
								 // Called on hot path: append one decision to the batch
								 // No serialization — just append to columnar builders
								 void append(const TransactionView& txn,
								 float risk_score,
								 int8_t decision,
								 int64_t latency_us) {
								 transaction_id_builder_->Append(txn.transaction_id.data,
								 txn.transaction_id.length);
								 amount_builder_->Append(txn.parsed_amount);
								 risk_score_builder_->Append(risk_score);
								 decision_builder_->Append(decision);
								 latency_us_builder_->Append(latency_us);
								
								 count_++;
								 if (count_ >= batch_size_) {
								 flush();
								 }
								 }
								
								 // Flush: finalize Arrow RecordBatch and publish via IPC
								 void flush() {
								 if (count_ == 0) return;
								
								 // Finalize arrays (zero-copy — builders own the memory)
								 auto batch = arrow::RecordBatch::Make(
								 schema_,
								 count_,
								 {
								 transaction_id_builder_->Finish().ValueOrDie(),
								 amount_builder_->Finish().ValueOrDie(),
								 risk_score_builder_->Finish().ValueOrDie(),
								 decision_builder_->Finish().ValueOrDie(),
								 latency_us_builder_->Finish().ValueOrDie(),
								 }
								 );
								
								 // Publish via Arrow IPC (shared memory or socket)
								 // Downstream consumers read the same memory — ZERO copy
								 auto stream = arrow::io::FixedSizeBufferWriter(
								 shared_mem_buffer_);
								 auto writer = arrow::ipc::MakeStreamWriter(
								 &stream, schema_).ValueOrDie();
								 writer->WriteRecordBatch(*batch);
								 writer->Close();
								
								 // Reset builders for next batch
								 count_ = 0;
								 // Builders reset internally on Finish()
								 }
								
								private:
								 std::shared_ptr<NumaMemoryPool> pool_;
								 size_t batch_size_;
								 size_t count_;
								 std::shared_ptr<arrow::Schema> schema_;
								 std::unique_ptr<arrow::StringBuilder> transaction_id_builder_;
								 std::unique_ptr<arrow::DoubleBuilder> amount_builder_;
								 std::unique_ptr<arrow::FloatBuilder> risk_score_builder_;
								 std::unique_ptr<arrow::Int8Builder> decision_builder_;
								 std::unique_ptr<arrow::Int64Builder> latency_us_builder_;
								 std::shared_ptr<arrow::Buffer> shared_mem_buffer_;
								};
